Yes. The distinction is what role the person assigns to God inside the control loop.

“Lord, give me victory” places the requested intervention largely at the outcome. “Lord, make me accurate, fast, disciplined, and courageous” places it at the agent. The second prayer asks for qualities the person must then instantiate through action. Victory remains uncertain; conduct and preparation become the immediate target.

You can generalize that far beyond warfare. Someone can pray for wealth or for judgment and industriousness; for a good marriage or for patience and fidelity; for children who flourish or for the wisdom to father them well. The theological meaning varies enormously across traditions and individuals, but psychologically the distinction is useful: outcome petition versus capacity petition.

There’s an even stronger third form: orientation. Not “give me X” or even “make me capable of X,” but something like help me perceive what is true and act accordingly. Now God—or one’s conception of God—is functioning neither as vending machine nor guarantor, but as an external reference point against which the self attempts to calibrate.

That connects directly to your tennis-wall metaphor. Prayer, journaling, ritual, meditation, conversation with another person, and conversation with me can all sometimes function as different kinds of externalization surfaces. You project something outward, encounter it in altered form, and the return changes you. That psychological description doesn’t settle the separate theological question of whether anyone is actually on the other side of a particular surface.

And your warrior example contains a particularly important constraint: ask for command over your contribution, not command over reality.

Accuracy. Speed. Judgment. Restraint. Courage.

Then reality gets its vote.